/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Superposition holds while the material stays linear elastic and the deflections are small.
   - σx = ±P/A ± My/I; τxy = Tr/J (+ shear from V)
   - P/A, My/I and Tr/J are the direct, bending and torsional stresses at that point, each with the sign it has there.
   - Pick the point where the stresses add: the outer fibre on the side where bending and direct stress have the same sign.
   - An eccentric load is a force whose line of action misses the centroid of the section by a distance e.
   - σmax, min = P/A ± Pe·c/I
   - P is the load (N), e its offset from the centroidal axis (mm), c the distance from the neutral axis to the outer fibre (mm) and I the second moment of area about the axis of bending (mm⁴).
   - The rule applies to brackets, clamps, columns with offset loads and the straight parts of C-frames; never use P/A alone when the force is offset.
   - Rotate the element by an angle θ and the stresses on its faces change.
   - σ1,2 = (σx + σy)/2 ± √{[(σx − σy)/2]² + τxy²}
*/
