/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - In a straight beam all fibres between two sections have the same length, so the change in length, the strain and the stress all grow linearly with the distance from the neutral axis.
   - With no net axial force from bending, the tension and compression must balance, which pulls the neutral axis toward the centre of curvature.
   - Rn = A/∫(dA/r); rectangle: Rn = h/ln(Ro/Ri); e = R − Rn
   - Ri and Ro are the inner and outer radii (mm); h = Ro − Ri is the depth and b the width of the rectangle (mm); A = bh is the area (mm²); R = Ri + h/2 is the radius of the centroidal axis (mm).
   - σb = M(Rn − r)/(A·e·r)
   - σb is the bending stress (MPa) at a fibre of radius r (mm); M is the bending moment about the centroidal axis (N·mm), taken positive when it tends to straighten (open) the beam, as a load does on a hook.
   - At the inner fibre r = Ri: σbi = M(Rn − Ri)/(A·e·Ri), tension.
   - In a crane hook the critical section is the horizontal section through the centre of curvature, the deepest part of the bend.
   - In a C-frame (a press, a riveter, a G-clamp) the load acts in the jaw at a distance a from the centre of curvature of the curved back, so M = P(a + R) about the centroidal axis of the curved section.
*/
