/* book Worked example 2: Q: The curved back of a C-frame has a rectangular section 40 mm wide and 100 mm deep with an inner radius of 100 mm. A 20 kN load acts in the jaws along a line 150 mm from the centre of curvature. Find the resultant stresses at the inner and outer fibres.
   1. Ro = 200 mm; A = 40 × 100 = 4000 mm²; R = 150 mm.
   2. Rn = 100/ln(200/100) = 100/0.6931 = 144.27 mm; e = 150 − 144.27 = 5.73 mm.
   3. M = P(a + R) = 20,000 × (150 + 150) = 6.0 × 10⁶ N·mm; P/A = 5.0 MPa.
   4. Inner fibre: σbi = 6.0 × 10⁶ × 44.27/(4000 × 5.73 × 100) = 115.9 MPa; resultant 120.9 MPa tension.
   5. Outer fibre: σbo = −6.0 × 10⁶ × 55.73/(4000 × 5.73 × 200) = −72.9 MPa; resultant −67.9 MPa.
   6. Straight beam for comparison: Z = 40 × 100²/6 = 66,667 mm³, M/Z = 90.0 MPa.
   Answer: Inner fibre 120.9 MPa tension, outer fibre 67.9 MPa compression; the inner bending peak of 115.9 MPa is 29 % above the straight-beam value of 90.0 MPa. */
